    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed some of the shards have these very irregular, almost jagged edges that look like they were shattered under intense heat, whereas others seem more cleanly broken. Does the specific way a piece fractures—the fracture morphology—tell us anything about the internal stress from the firing process or the cooling stage?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, the fracture morphology can provide important clues about the firing and cooling stages. Fracture patterns caused by sudden, intense heat stress on a piece—often known as craze marks or crazing—can indicate rapid firing rates or thermal shock. Meanwhile, smooth or conchoidal breaks suggest consistent cooling. High thermal gradients might result in notched or splintered edges, indicative of uneven cooling. Collectively, these features can suggest differences in kiln conditions and cooling practices used historically, offering indirect insight into the technological choices of the potters.",
      "source": "target",
